# Arrows or potential outcomes? Bad controls and colliders
next: {"title":"Difference-in-differences and the parallel trends assumption","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/difference-in-differences-and-the-parallel-trends-assumption.cast"}

## Why Admission Is a Collider
voice: male
level: advanced
use: causal_dag
with:
    nodes:
      - {name: Insurance D, role: exposure}
      - {name: Health Y, role: outcome}
      - {name: Sickness U, role: confounder}
      - {name: Admitted A, role: collider}
    edges: Insurance D -> Health Y; Sickness U -> Health Y; Insurance D -> Admitted A; Sickness U -> Admitted A

    text claim_a "Claim 1: among the admitted, severity is held fixed." #2f6b8f x 500 y 715 font_size 22 hidden true
    text claim_b "Claim 2: admission is a common effect — conditioning invents an association." #b5482e x 500 y 678 font_size 22 hidden true
    mark mark_claim #b5482e target claim_a kind strike hidden true
    path tbl_frame closed #8f887c points [[240,300],[660,300],[660,500],[240,500]] hidden true
    path tbl_v #8f887c points [[450,300],[450,500]] hidden true
    path tbl_h #8f887c points [[240,400],[660,400]] hidden true
    text tbl_caption "Admitted patients only (A_i = 1)" x 450 y 560 font_size 23 hidden true
    text head_d1 "Insured (D=1)" #2f6b8f x 345 y 520 font_size 21 hidden true
    text head_d0 "Uninsured (D=0)" #b5482e x 555 y 520 font_size 21 hidden true
    text head_u1 "Sick U=1" x 150 y 448 font_size 21 hidden true
    text head_u0 "Healthy U=0" x 150 y 348 font_size 21 hidden true
    text c_sick_ins "1,200" x 345 y 443 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c_sick_un "800" x 555 y 443 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c_h_ins "1,600" x 345 y 343 font_size 26 hidden true
    text c_h_un "400" x 555 y 343 font_size 26 hidden true
    text share_lab "Share sick" x 150 y 263 font_size 21 hidden true
    text share_ins "43%" #2f6b8f x 345 y 263 font_size 24 hidden true
    text share_un "67%" #b5482e x 555 y 263 font_size 24 hidden true
    text mean_lab "Mean health Y" x 150 y 213 font_size 21 hidden true
    text mean_ins "60.9" #2f6b8f x 345 y 213 font_size 24 hidden true
    text mean_un "53.3" #b5482e x 555 y 213 font_size 24 hidden true
    text gap_text "Observed gap: +7.5" x 825 y 460 font_size 22 hidden true
    text truth_text "True effect: −2" #b5482e x 825 y 400 font_size 22 hidden true

Now that we have seen a control that makes the comparison worse, here is the defence of it: among hospital patients, severity is roughly held fixed.
    draw claim_a

And here is the objection: admission is caused by both coverage and sickness, so conditioning on it manufactures a link that was never in the population.
    draw claim_b

Back to Oregon: D_i is insurance coverage, handed out by lottery, Y_i is the health outcome, and this arrow is the effect we want.
    draw node_insurance_d node_health_y edge_0

Unobserved sickness U_i drives health directly — and because the lottery assigned D_i, U_i is independent of coverage in the full sample.
    draw node_sickness_u edge_1

    pause 0.3

Admission A_i sits downstream of both: coverage lowers the price of care and raises admission, and being sick raises it too.
    draw node_admitted_a edge_2 edge_3

D to A to U is a path with two arrowheads colliding at A — and a collider blocks its own path, so left alone nothing leaks through it.
    focus node_insurance_d node_admitted_a node_sickness_u edge_2 edge_3

Conditioning on A_i opens it: once admission is fixed, learning that someone is insured tells you something about how sick they must have been to get that bed.
    highlight node_admitted_a effect glow

Epidemiologists met this in 1946, when Joseph Berkson showed that studying only hospital patients can link diseases that are entirely unrelated in the general population.

    pause 0.5

    clear keep ["claim_a","claim_b"]

    draw tbl_frame tbl_v tbl_h

Put numbers on it: ten thousand insured, ten thousand uninsured, exactly two thousand genuinely sick in each arm — then keep only those admitted to hospital.
    draw head_d1 head_d0 head_u1 head_u0 tbl_caption

Of the two thousand sick, 1,200 of the insured are admitted against 800 of the uninsured — coverage buys access.
    draw c_sick_ins c_sick_un

Among the eight thousand healthy, coverage matters proportionally even more: 1,600 admissions versus 400.
    draw c_h_ins c_h_un

Add the columns and the balance is gone: 43 percent of admitted insured are sick, against 67 percent of the admitted uninsured — a negative D–U association the lottery never created.
    draw share_lab share_ins share_un

    pause 0.4

So mean health among the admitted is 60.9 for the insured and 53.3 for the uninsured: seven and a half points in insurance's favour.
    draw mean_lab mean_ins mean_un gap_text

Yet in this data-generating process coverage lowers the index by two points in every cell — the control did not clean the comparison, it reversed its sign.
    draw truth_text mark_claim delivery grave

In potential-outcomes language: among the admitted, Y_i(0) averages 62.9 for the insured and 53.3 for the uninsured, so Y_i(0) is no longer independent of D_i.
    text po_line "E[Y_i(0) | D_i=1, A_i=1] = 62.9   ≠   E[Y_i(0) | D_i=0, A_i=1] = 53.3" x 500 y 120 font_size 24
    mark mark_po target po_line kind box

Randomization balances the whole sample; it promises nothing inside a subsample selected by a variable that treatment itself moves.

## What the arrow sees and what it hides
voice: male
level: advanced

    text claim1 "\"Arrows are just decoration.\"" x 250 y 705 font_size 24 hidden true
    mark strike1 target claim1 kind strike hidden true
    path divider dashed #8f887c points [[500,665],[500,400]] hidden true
    text claim2 "\"Potential outcomes are bookkeeping.\"" x 745 y 705 font_size 22 hidden true
    mark strike2 target claim2 kind strike hidden true

Now that we have seen why admission is a collider, ask which language would have caught it first — some economists say the arrows are decoration.
    draw claim1

Others say the potential-outcomes algebra is mere bookkeeping around a picture; let's put both to work on the same regression.
    draw divider claim2

On the left, the graph: coverage D, spending Y, and the arrow we are actually after.
    circle node_D "Coverage D" x 150 y 600
    circle node_Y "Spending Y" x 430 y 600
    edge edge_DY node_D -> node_Y #2f6b8f

    pause 0.3

Unobserved severity U also drives spending — harmless in itself, since a lottery, not severity, decides who is offered coverage.
    circle node_U "Severity U" x 430 y 450
    edge edge_UY node_U -> node_Y #8a5fa8

But both of them push on admission: coverage makes a hospital stay more likely, and so does being sicker.
    circle node_A "Admitted A" x 150 y 450
    edge edge_DA node_D -> node_A #b5482e
    edge edge_UA node_U -> node_A #b5482e

    pause 0.3

Boxing A is exactly what running the regression on admitted patients does.
    mark box_A target node_A kind box

And the verdict is read straight off the path: D into A, back out to U, on to Y.
    focus node_D node_A node_U node_Y edge_DA edge_UA edge_UY

On the right, the same restriction written in expectations: the difference in means among the admitted.
    text eq1 "E[Y_i | D_i=1, A_i=1] − E[Y_i | D_i=0, A_i=1]" x 745 y 610 font_size 20

Decompose it as in lecture two — the effect on the admitted, plus a gap in Y_i(0) between insured and uninsured admitted patients.
    text eq2 "= E[Y_i(1) − Y_i(0) | D_i=1, A_i=1]" x 745 y 555 font_size 20
    text eq3 "+ E[Y_i(0)|D_i=1,A_i=1] − E[Y_i(0)|D_i=0,A_i=1]" #b5482e x 745 y 505 font_size 18

Whether that second term vanishes is a fact about the world; the notation poses the question and then goes quiet.
    text eq_note "Is this term zero? The line cannot say." #8f887c x 745 y 445 font_size 20

So the first claim falls: the arrows deliver by inspection what the expectation can only state.
    draw strike1 delivery grave

    pause 0.5

    clear keep ["claim1","strike1","claim2"]

The warning itself is old: never control for an outcome of treatment, never condition on a collider — two dialects of one rule, and admission is flagged by both.
    text ap1 "Angrist–Pischke: never control for an outcome of treatment." x 500 y 600 font_size 21
    text ap2 "Pearl: never condition on a collider on a back-door path." x 500 y 555 font_size 21

    pause 0.3

Now ask the graph what it cannot draw: an arrow looks the same whether the effect is large for a few and zero for everyone else.
    text hide_head "What the arrows cannot draw" #b5482e x 500 y 470 font_size 26
    text item1 "1. Heterogeneity: whose effect is it?" x 500 y 405 font_size 21

In Oregon about thirty thousand names were drawn from ninety thousand sign-ups, and only about a quarter enrolled — compliers and monotonicity live in the potential outcomes, not on the page.
    text item2 "2. Monotonicity and compliers: 1 in 4 enrolled" x 500 y 350 font_size 21

And no graph carries a standard error: reading Oregon's null on blood sugar is a question of precision, not of paths.
    text item3 "3. Sampling variability: reading a null" x 500 y 295 font_size 21

So the second claim falls too — that bookkeeping is where heterogeneity, compliance and sampling variability are kept.
    draw strike2 delivery grave

The graph tells you which conditional expectation to look at; the potential outcomes tell you what it is an average of.
    text synth "The graph picks the expectation; the algebra says what it averages." #2f6b8f x 500 y 205 font_size 20

## Substance or Language?
voice: male
level: advanced

    box claim_a "A dispute about substance" #b5482e x 250 y 690 hidden true
    label claim_a_sub "The two frameworks make different causal claims" below #b5482e attach_to claim_a hidden true
    box claim_b "A dispute about language" #2f6b8f x 745 y 690 hidden true
    label claim_b_sub "Same mathematics, different cheap assumptions" below #2f6b8f attach_to claim_b hidden true
    mark cross_a target claim_a kind cross hidden true
    circle n_d "D" x 150 y 330 hidden true
    label lbl_d "Coverage (lottery)" below attach_to n_d hidden true
    circle n_y "Y" x 450 y 330 hidden true
    label lbl_y "Inpatient outcome" below attach_to n_y hidden true
    arrow e_dy n_d -> n_y hidden true
    circle n_u "U" #8f887c x 300 y 480 hidden true
    label lbl_u "Severity (unobserved)" above #8f887c attach_to n_u hidden true
    arrow e_uy n_u -> n_y #8f887c hidden true
    circle n_a "A" #8a5fa8 x 300 y 180 hidden true
    label lbl_a "Admitted to hospital" below #8a5fa8 attach_to n_a hidden true
    arrow e_da n_d -> n_a #8a5fa8 hidden true
    arrow e_ua n_u -> n_a #8a5fa8 hidden true
    label lbl_da "+30% admissions, year 1" left #b5482e attach_to e_da hidden true

Now that we have seen what an arrow reveals and what it hides, the last question decides whether you must pick a side: some insist the Pearl–Rubin quarrel is substantive, that the two frameworks make genuinely different causal claims.
    draw claim_a claim_a_sub

Others insist it is purely linguistic — identical mathematics, differing only in which assumptions each notation makes cheap to state.
    draw claim_b claim_b_sub

Judge it on our running case: Oregon drew about thirty thousand names from ninety thousand on the list, so D_i, coverage, is randomized, and Y_i is an inpatient outcome.
    draw n_d n_y e_dy lbl_d lbl_y

    pause 0.3

Severity sits in no dataset, and it moves the outcome on its own.
    draw n_u e_uy lbl_u

Hospital admission is caused by both — coverage raises it, severity raises it — so admission is a collider.
    draw n_a e_da e_ua lbl_a

Analysing only the admitted conditions on that collider, and inside the stratum the two parents become dependent.
    focus n_a e_da e_ua

    pause 0.4

Now the same setup in the other language: the lottery buys independence of the potential outcomes from treatment.
    text po_1 "{Y_i(1), Y_i(0)} ⟂ D_i" x 745 y 420 font_size 26
    text po_1_note "what the lottery buys" #8f887c x 745 y 382 font_size 19

And conditioning on admission is precisely the failure of that same independence, given A equals one.
    text po_2 "{Y_i(1), Y_i(0)} ⟂ D_i | A_i = 1" x 745 y 300 font_size 24
    text po_2_note "✗ fails — A is a common effect of D and U" #8a5fa8 x 745 y 262 font_size 19

Every d-separation claim in the graph is a conditional-independence claim about potential outcomes — nothing here is true in one framework and false in the other, so the substantive reading does not survive.
    draw cross_a delivery grave

What differs is economy: the graph makes "which controls are safe" nearly free to state, while potential outcomes make "which average, for whom" nearly free to state — and each framework tends to under-emphasise the question the other answers cheaply.
    erase claim_a claim_a_sub claim_b claim_b_sub cross_a

That gives a protocol. Draw the graph for one job only: choosing the conditioning set — which covariates block back-door paths, and which open collider paths.
    box prot_dag "Draw the DAG" #8a5fa8 x 250 y 690
    label prot_dag_sub "Choose the conditioning set" below #8a5fa8 attach_to prot_dag

Then write potential outcomes for the other job: naming the estimand — intention-to-treat, or a LATE for lottery compliers, and the population it is defined on.
    box prot_po "Write potential outcomes" x 745 y 690
    label prot_po_sub "Define the estimand" below attach_to prot_po

Apply it to Oregon's admitted patients: in the first year coverage raised hospital admissions by roughly thirty percent, so that arrow into A is real and large.
    draw lbl_da

The admitted insured therefore include marginal patients who would never have been admitted without coverage; the admitted uninsured are, on average, sicker.
    highlight n_a effect glow

Inside that subsample the lottery's independence is gone, and no covariate adjustment restores it, because the selection ran through a post-treatment variable.
    text punch "Among the admitted, D_i ⟂ Y_i(1), Y_i(0) no longer holds" #b5482e x 500 y 75 font_size 24
    mark punch_box target punch kind box

Across the four parts — the control that hurts, the collider behind it, the arrow that exposes it — the graph tells you what to condition on, potential outcomes tell you what you are estimating, and the whole dispute is only about which of those questions your notation asks first.

